## Introduction
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs), a kind of crystalline porous materials constructed by connecting metal ions/clusters and bridging organic ligands via coordination bonds,[1] have been extensively studied for applications in catalysis,[2] gas storage and separation,[3,4] drug delivery[5] and sensing[6] owing to their tailorable structure and function, ultrahigh porosity and large surface area. Although many promising methods have been applied to prepare multifunctional MOFs, such as modulating crystal structure[7,8] and compounding with other functional materials,[2,9] their performance still needs great improvement. Since the discovery of graphene, two-dimensional (2D) materials, which possess the characteristics of large surface area and relatively high surface energy, have been found that the unique physicochemical properties cannot be achieved by their bulk counterparts.[10,11]
2D MOFs, as a newly 2D material, possessing many promising features have drawn tremendous attention recently. As compared to the conventional 3D bulk MOFs crystals, 2D MOFs possess larger surface area and more accessible active sites which can reduce diffusion barriers, facilitate the contact of substrates with the active sites and afford rapid mass transport and charge transfer, endowing them enhanced performance in catalysis applications.[12-18] Unfortunately, to date, it is still a great challenge to easily fabricate appropriate 2D MOF nanosheets in high yields through a simple process. Conventionally, two major strategies, top-down and bottomup have been applied to synthesize 2D MOFs nanosheets.[13] The topdown approach, such as sonication exfoliation[19] and chemical intercalation,[20] has been applied to delaminate the layered 3D bulk MOF precursors to nanosheets. This strategy is simple but it often results in the prepared nanostructure suffer from structural deterioration, fragmentation and aggregation, and it also led to low yield and difficulty in morphology control.[18,21,22] The bottom-up approach for preparation of 2D MOFs nanosheets, including threelayer synthesis[16], surfactant-assisted method[23] and capping method,[24,25] is more desirable to obtain high-aspect-ratio nanosheets in high yield. However, as far as we know, there were no reports about the method to controllably regulate the thickness of 2D MOFs nanosheets. In order to study the influence of thickness on performances, it is desired that new methods would be developed to obtain the high-yield and well-dispersed 2D MOF nanosheets with different thickness in a fast and controllable process. In addition, it is necessary to explore the influence of different metal nodes on the catalytic activity of MOFs because metal nodes play an important role in the catalytic process.
Herein, for the first time, a bottom-up synthetic strategy without surfactants was well developed for the rapid synthesis of porphyrinbased 2D lanthanide MOFs (Ln-TCPP) in household microwave oven, and the nanosheets with different thickness were successfully obtained by controlling the acetic acid concentration (Figure S1). The as-prepared 2D Ln-MOFs nanosheets show thickness and metal node-dependent photocatalytic performance (Scheme 1). The higher Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area, light harvesting ability, carrier density and the electron (e)-hole (h) separation efficiency in thinner nanosheets donate their higher photocatalytic activity towards photooxidation of 1,5-dihydroxynaphthalene (1,5-DHN) to synthesize juglone. Remarkably, the constructed 2D Yb-TCPP nanosheets displays higher photodynamic activity to produce $\bar{\mathsf{O}}_{2}\bar{\mathsf{\Lambda}}$ and $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ than assynthesized other 2D lanthanide MOFs owing to the effectively energy and electron transfer between TCPP and $\Upsilon\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ , thus greatly improving the photocatalytic activity. This work demonstrates that the ultrathin structure and the energy/electron transfer between ligand and metal nodes will provide additional opportunities to enhance photocatalytic performances of MOFs.
## RESEARCH ARTICLE
## Results and Discussion
Taking 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets as an example, the scanning electron microscope (SEM), transmission electron microscope (TEM) and atomic force microscope (AFM) images indicated that the 2D TbTCPP nanosheets with different thickness could be easily tuned by controlling the dosage of acetic acid (commonly used as a modulator to inhibit the deprotonation of carboxyl in ligand to obtain large single crystals[26,27]) (Figure 1 and Figure S2 ). The bright spots were clearly presented in selected-area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern, which were attributed to (020), (021) and (041) planes of 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets, confirming the crystal structure (inset in Figure 1a). Correspondingly, the thickness was changed from $1.64\:\mathsf{n m}$ to 45.33 nm with the increase of acetic acid dosage (Figure 1b), and the thickness was positively correlated with acetic acid concentration (Figure S3). In the X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) of 2D TbTCPP nanosheets, the peaks at 531.8 eV and 532.8 eV assigned to the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}={\mathsf{O}}$ and C-OH groups of the O 1s in ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{T C P P}}$ were shifted to 531.9 eV and $533.3~\mathsf{e V}$ , respectively (Figure S4b). Meanwhile, the two peaks of Tb 3d at $1243.0~\mathsf{e V}$ and 1277.7 eV were also shifted to 1242.1 eV and 1277.0 eV, respectively (Figure S4c), which demonstrated the successful coordination of the carboxyl groups in ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{T C P P}}$ ligand with $\mathsf{T b}^{3+}$ nodes. However, no obvious shift can be observed in N 1s spectrum of 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets as compared with that of H2TCPP (Figure S4d), indicating porphyrin ring is not metallized by ${\mathsf{T}}{\mathsf{b}}^{3+}$ . Moreover, the Raman spectra of 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets with different thickness show typical characteristic of ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{T C P P}}$ except for the peak at $476\ c m^{-1}$ which is assigned to the TbO (Figure S5).[28-30] In the FTIR spectra (Figure S6), the shoulder peaks at $1689~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ and $1604~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ assigned to the asymmetric stretching vibration of $C=0$ in ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{T C P P}}$ were shifted to $1581~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ and $1539\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ respectively, and significantly decreased in intensity for 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets. The disappearance of stretching vibration of C-O at 1261 cm-1 can be also observed in the spectra of 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets. All these results demonstrate that the 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets are constructed by the strong coordination of $\mathsf{T b}^{3+}$ and the carboxyl groups in ${\sf H}_{2}^{,}$ TCPP.
The BET surface areas of 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets increase obviously with the thickness decrease (Figure S8), from $53.3~\mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{g}$ to $106.1\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/9\$ , mostly ascribed to the exposed external surface of these isolated or separated nanosheets,[22,32,33] which is conducive to improve catalytic performance. The pore size distribution curves (Figure S8, inset) indicate that the 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets have similar micropores of $1.23\ \mathsf{n m}$ , which is consistent with the value of $1.37\ \mathsf{n m}$ based on crystallographic data. All the 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets with difference thickness have similar thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) curves (Figure S9). A weight loss of $\sim17\%$ in the range of $20-350~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , which could be attributed to the loss of coordinated solvent molecule. The following dramatic weight-loss that happened at $500^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ corresponded to the decomposition of TCPP ligands within the framework, indicating the de-solvated framework starts to collapse from $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . These data demonstrate that the nanosheets with difference thickness exhibit considerable thermal stability up to approximately $500^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . The UV-vis spectra of 2D TbTCPP nanosheets revealed that the Soret band at $416\ \mathsf{n m}$ assigned to characteristic peak of porphyrin ring exhibited a slight red-shift in the 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets as compared with the free ligand ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{T C P P}}$ (Figure S10a). Meanwhile, the corresponding maximum absorption wavelength was shifted from $430\ \mathsf{n m}$ to $420\ \mathsf{n m}$ with thickness decrease (Figure S 10b). The four distinct $\mathsf{Q}$ bands absorption peaks can be observed, further demonstrating the porphyrin centre is not filled with $\mathsf{T b}^{3+}$ .[34] The UV−vis spectra of 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets showed the maximum absorbance and absorption coefficient of the corresponding Soret band increased gradually along with the thickness decrease, revealing that the thinner the thickness, the stronger light harvesting ability (Figure S10c and 10d)[20], which was further demonstrated by the optical transmission spectra and solid absorption spectra of 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets (Figure S11 and Figure S12). Besides, the Uv-vis absorption spectra and transmission spectra of prepared FTO photoelectrodes also manifest that the thinner the thickness of 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets, the stronger light capture ability (Figure S13), which may result in a higher photocurrent response. Investigations of the diffuse reflectance spectra of 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets with different thickness showed that the energy gaps $(\mathsf{E}_{9})$ estimated by Tauc plots are $1.65\:\mathrm{eV}$ , 1.68 eV, 1.70 eV and 1.72 eV for Tb-TCPP-1, Tb-TCPP-2, Tb-TCPP-3 and Tb-TCPP-4 respectively (Figure S14), revealing the thicknessdependent bandgap of 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets. Besides, cyclic
voltammetry measurements showed that the $\mathsf{E}_{9}^{\mathsf{e c}}$ between HOMO and LUMO are consistent with the energy gaps $(\mathsf E_{9}^{\mathsf{o p t}})$ determined from Tauc plots (Figure S15 and Table S2), further manifesting that the bandgaps of 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets are closely related to the thickness. Meanwhile, the first-principles calculations that performed by the density-functional theory (DFT) with the single-crystal structure indicated that the 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets exhibit a typical semiconducting behavior with the bandgap of $1.78\:\mathrm{eV}$ (Figure S16), which is consistent with the experimental value determined from the Tauc plots and cyclic voltammetry curves. To elucidate the semiconductor behavior of 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets with different thickness, the Mott−Schottky measurements on Tb-TCPP were conducted at frequencies of 1000, 1500, and $2000~{\mathsf{H}}z$ (Figure S17). The positive slopes of Mott−Schottky plots for 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets with different thickness are consistent with the behavior of typical n-type semiconductor. Moreover, at the fixed frequency of 1 $000{\mathsf{H z}}$ , the carrier density of Tb-TCPP-4 is 2.17, 2.13 and 1.31 times higher than those of Tb-TCPP-1, Tb-TCPP-2 and Tb-TCPP-3, respectively (details see the description below Figure S17), demonstrating that the thinner the thickness, the higher photogeneration electron density.
The aforementioned results prove that the microwave-assisted strategy has been successfully performed to synthesize 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets with different thickness by controlling the acetic acid concentration. More importantly, this method was also successfully applied to prepare various kinds of 2D Ln-TCPP ( $\scriptstyle\mathsf{L}\mathsf{n}=\mathsf{C}\mathsf{e}$ , Sm, Eu, Yb) nanosheets with different thickness. The XRD patterns of $20$ LnTCPP-4 $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Ln=Ce}$ , Sm, Eu, Yb) nanosheets are also almost identical with that of bulk Tb-TCPP MOFs and the simulated pattern, revealing the same crystal structure with that of the as-prepared 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets (Figure S18). The SEM images illuminated that the thickness of  2D Ln-TCPP $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Ln=Ce}$ , Sm, Eu, Yb) nanosheets also can be tailored by the dosage of acetic acid (Figure S19-S22). Meanwhile, the 2D Ln-TCPP-4 $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Ln=Ce}$ , Sm, Eu, Yb) nanosheets show similar BET surface and micropore size, which is in line with that of Tb-TCPP4 (Figure S23). In addition, the  similar UV–vis absorption spectra with that of 2D Tb-TCPP were observed (Figure S24), indicating their great light harvesting ability. The TGA curves (Figure S25) revealed the high thermal stability of as-perpared Ln-TCPP-4 ( $\scriptstyle\mathsf{L}\mathsf{n}=\mathsf{C}\mathsf{e}$ , Sm, Eu,
Yb). The n-type semiconductor behaviors of 2D Ln-TCPP $(L n=C e$ , Sm, Eu, Yb) nanosheets were also confirmed by Mott−Schottky plots, and reflecting the thickness-dependent carrier density in the meantime (Figure S26-S29).
As well known, porphyrin-based MOFs are extensively used for $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ generation in photocatalysis and photodynamics therapy.[21,35-37] In order to clarify whether $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ species were actually generated in the photocatalytic process, 9,10-diphenylanthracene (DPA) was used as a $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ probe to evaluate the $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ generation ability of 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets under visible light irradiation. The characteristic absorption of DPA at $374{\mathsf{n m}}$ decreased in the presence 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets, revealing the formation of $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ (Figure S30).[38-40] Moreover, 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets with thinner thickness exhibit better performance in $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ generation (Figure 3a). Interestingly, the Yb-TCPP-4 shows outstanding $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ generation ability as compared with other Ln-TCPP-4 (Figure 3b and Figure S31). To assess the $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ quantum yield, Rose Bengal (RB) was used as a standard photosensitizer ( $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ quantum yield of $\phi_{\mathsf{R B}}=0.86$ in ethanol)[41-43]. By comparing the peak areas of $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ emission at around $1270\:\mathrm{nm}$ , the $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ quantum yield was determined to be 0.42, 0.45, 0.48, 0.55 for TbTCPP-1, Tb-TCPP-2, Tb-TCPP-3, Tb-TCPP-4, respectively, and the Yb-TCPP-4 shows the highest $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ quantum yield up to 0.63 (Figure S 32, Table S3 ). Besides, the electron spin resonance (ESR) spectra show typical 1:1:1 triplet signals of TEMPO nitroxide radical, also indicating the formation of $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ upon light irradiation (Figure S33a). Meanwhile, the stronger TEMPO signal performed on Yb-TCPP-4 suggested the stronger  ability to produce $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ of Yb-TCPP-4. Amazingly, when dimethyl pyridine N-oxide (DMPO) as trapping agent, the ESR spectra showed a typical signals of $D M P O{-}O_{2}^{-}$ (Figure S33b), demonstrating the generation of $\bigcirc_{2}\cdot$ . It also showed the stronger the ability to produce $\bar{\mathsf{O}}_{2}\bar{\mathsf{\Pi}}$ of Yb-TCPP-4. However, the ESR spectra did not show the DMPO-•OH signals, suggesting no formation of •OH in the system (Figure S33c).
For the proof-of-concept application, the 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets with different thickness were performed to photooxidation of 1,5-DHN to synthesize juglone. The absorption at 417 nm was observed in the presence of 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets, demontrating the formation of juglone (Figure S34)[21]. Besides, the thinner the 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets, the higher the photocatalytic performance towards the photooxidation of 1,5-DHN (Figure 3c). The photooxidation experiments of 1,5-DHN catalyzed by 2D Ln-TCPP-4 nanosheets demonstrated that the Yb-TCPP-4 exhibit the best photocatalytic activity (Figure 3d and Figure S35). The SEM and PXRD results of Ln-TCPP-4 after photocatalytic reaction demonstrated the good stability of the nanosheets in the reaction process (Figure S36). Besides, there was no significant loss of catalytic activity in the five successive catalytic cycles, suggesting that the Ln-TCPP-4 possesses stable catalytic activity (Figure S37).
To elucidate the mechanism of thickness-dependent photocatalytic activity and the exceptional activity of Yb-TCPP-4, the photocurrent measurements have been also performed to unveil their e-h separation efficiency. As expected, the photocurrent density increased with the decrease of the thickness of 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets, indicating the thinner the thickness, the higher charge separation efficiency (Figure 4a and 4b), which may lead to produce more $\mathsf{O}_{2}^{-}$ . According to all the above results, 2D Tb-TCPP nanosheets with thinner thickness show higher BET surface areas, light harvesting ability, carrier density and the e-h separation efficiency, which can effectively enhance photocatalytic performance. Surprisedly, the YbTCPP-4 shows the highest photocurrent response among all investigated Ln-TCPP-4 (Figure 4c and 4d), illustrating that the highest e-h separation efficiency. However, the Ln-TCPP-4 displays similar absorption spectra (Figure S38), revealing no obvious difference in light-harvesting ability. Moreover, the 2D Ln-TCPP-4 $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Ln=Ce}$ , Sm, Eu, Yb) show the similar BET surface area (Figure S23) and energy gaps (Figure S39) with that of 2D Tb-TCPP-4 nanosheets.
Therefore, in order to further clarify the mechanism behind the exceptional activity of Yb-TCPP-4, the energy and electron transfer between TCPP and $L n^{3+}$ was considered. The singlet excited state (1TCPP) and triplet excited state $({}^{3}\mathsf{T C P P})$ of ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{T C P P}}$ were investigated by TD-DFT. The 1TCPP and 3TCPP were calculated as $18260~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ and $12310\ \mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ respectively (Table S4). According to Reinhoudt’s empirical rule,[44] the effective intersystem crossing process would be occurred when the energy gap between 1TCPP and 3TCPP of the ligand more than $5000\ c m^{-1}$ .[45] While the energy gap of ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{T C P P}}$ was $5950~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ , so ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{T C P P}}$ possesses high intersystem crossing efficiency. Besides, the effective energy transfer between ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{T C P P}}$ and $L n^{3+}$ also requires that ${}^{3}\mathsf{T C P P}$ matches well with the lowest emission level of $\mathsf{L}\mathsf{n}^{3+}$ .[46] However, the lowest emission levels of ${\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ , $\mathsf{S}\mathsf{m}^{3+}$ , $\mathsf{E u}^{3+}$ , $\mathsf{T b}^{3+}$ are all higher than 3TCPP, except for $\mathsf{Y b}^{3+}$ (Figure S40 and Table S4). Hence, the effective energy transfer between TCPP and $\Upsilon\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ would happen. As direct evidence, the fluorescence spectra of Ln-TCPP-4 ( $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Ln=Ce}$ , Sm, Eu, Tb) only show the characteristic emission of TCPP, while the reduced fluorescence intensity of TCPP and the intense near infrared emission of $\Upsilon\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ in Yb-TCPP-4 further validated the effective energy transfer between TCPP and $\mathsf{Y b}^{3+}$ (Figure S41). Of course, the fluorescence quenching may also be caused by effective electron transfer between TCPP and $\Upsilon\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ .[47]
According to above results, we supposed a possible mechanism to elucidate the superior activity of Yb-TCPP-4. As Figure 5 shown, the effective energy transfer (Dexter energy transfer) between TCPP and $\Upsilon\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ may facilitate the intersystem crossing from 1TCPP to ${}^{3}\mathsf{T C P P}$ through spin-orbit coupling.[48,49] Moreover, because of the small energy gap between the ${}^{3}\mathsf{T C P P}$ and ${}^{2}\mathsf{F}_{5/2}$ of $\Upsilon\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ , thermally activated back energy transfer (BEnT) from ${^2\mathsf{F}}_{5/2}$ of $\forall6^{3+}$ to 3TCPP may occur, which could extend the ${}^{3}\mathsf{T C P P}$ lifetime.[46,50,51] Therefore, the efficient intersystem crossing and prolonged triplet-state lifetime of TCPP facilitate the generation of $^10_{2}$ through type Ⅱ mechanism.[52] Meanwhile, because of the ${}^{2}\mathsf{F}_{5/2}$ energy level of $\mathsf{Y b}^{3+}$ is lower than ${\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ (5d), $\mathsf{S m}^{3+}(^{4}\mathsf{G}_{5/2}),\mathsf{E u}^{3+}(^{5}\mathsf{D}_{0}),\mathsf{T b}^{3+}(^{5}\mathsf{D}_{4}),$ and macthed well with TCPP, the $\Upsilon\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ as deep trap state can effectively capture photogenerated electrons on 1TCPP level[47,53,54], which can effectively suppressed the e-h recombination, and thus promoting the formation of $\bar{\mathsf{O}}_{2}\bar{\mathsf{\Pi}}$ through type Ⅰ mechanism. In comparison, the photogenerated electrons cannot be effectively traped by $\mathsf{C e}^{3+}/\mathsf{S m}^{3+}/\mathsf{E u}^{3+}/\mathsf{T b}^{3+}$ as shallow trap state, leading to fast back electron transfer from metal-cluster to 1TCPP level.
## Conclusion
In summary, we have successfully developed a series of porphyrin-based 2D lanthanide MOFs with different thickness as potential photocatalyst via microwave-assisted stratagy. The asprepared 2D Ln-TCPP nanosheets with  typical n-type semiconductor behavior and thickness-dependent photocatalytic activity were successfully used as photocatalysts for photooxidation of 1,5-DHN. The research on mechanism demonstrates that the thinner the thickness, the higher BET surface area, light harvesting ability, carrier density and the e-h separation efficiency, thus endowing them better photocatalytic activity. Miraculously, the Yb-TCPP-4 showed an oustanding photocatalytic performance among the investigated 2D Ln-TCPP-4 owing to the effective energy and electron transfer beween TCPP and $\forall6^{3+}$ . This work not only lights a new beacon for the controllable synthesis of 2D MOFs but also offers a novel perspective for photocatalysis property tailoring.
